Hi Kathryn,

On 11-09-22 09:41 AM, Martin Morgan wrote:
> On 09/22/2011 09:15 AM, Kathryn Iverson wrote:
>> Hi Gord,
>> Thanks for the information, although I'm not quite sure how to proceed.
>> My understanding is I need to compile a shared library libbam and put
>> it in
>> /usr/local/lib64. Is the source for this library in Rsamtools itself?
>> I've
>> untared Rsamtools and am looking around for some kind of makefile but
>> can't
>> seem to find it. I see something in src/Makvars but there is no target
>> for
>> dylib.
>
>
> As a workaround I *think* you can edit src/Makevars.common to read
>
> PKG_LIBS += \
> $(SHLIB_OPENMP_CFLAGS) \
> -Llibbam.a -Llibbcf.a -Llibtabix.a
>
> instead of
>
> PKG_LIBS += \
> $(SHLIB_OPENMP_CFLAGS) \
> -L. -lbam -lbcf -ltabix

Alternatively, you could just uninstall the samtools that has been
compiled and installed under /usr/local/. Rsamtools contains its own
copy of the samtools source code so having an external installation
of samtools is not needed.

H.

>
> Martin
>
>>
>> Thanks again,
>> Kathryn
>>
>> On Thu, Sep 22, 2011 at 11:52 AM, Gordon Brown
>> <Gordon.Brown at cancer.org.uk>wrote:
>>
>>> Hi, Kathryn,
>>>
>>> Your copy of libbam (libbam.a) is a static library, not a shared library
>>> (and you can't, in general, link to a static library when trying to
>>> make a
>>> shared library). If you compile the shared version of libbam ("make
>>> dylib"
>>> in the samtools package will create "libbam.so.1"), and put it in
>>> /usr/local/lib64, Rsamtools should find that instead and use it.
>>>
>>> Hope this helps.
>>>
>>> Cheers,
>>>
>>> - Gord
